<BaseLayout
  title="Orthopaedic & Spine Conditions | Dr. Aayush Shrestha Nepal"
  description="Explore spine, knee, hip, shoulder, hand, foot, arthritis and trauma conditions assessed by an orthopaedic and spine surgeon in Nepal."
  canonical="https://draayushshrestha.com/conditions.html"
  active="conditions"
>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="shell page-hero"><p class="kicker">Conditions &amp; symptoms</p><h1>Find the right starting point.</h1><p>This directory groups common musculoskeletal symptoms and diagnoses. Similar pain can come from different causes, so an examination may be needed.</p></section>
    <section class="section section--tight"><div class="shell directory">
      <article class="directory__item" id="spine"><h2>Back &amp; spine</h2><ul><li>Lower-back and upper-back pain</li><li>Sciatica and radiculopathy</li><li>Herniated or bulging discs</li><li>Spinal stenosis</li><li>Spondylosis and spondylolisthesis</li><li>Scoliosis and kyphosis</li><li>Spine infection, tumour or trauma</li></ul></article>
      <article class="directory__item" id="neck"><h2>Neck</h2><ul><li>Neck and shoulder-blade pain</li><li>Arm pain, numbness or tingling</li><li>Cervical disc problems</li><li>Cervical spondylosis</li><li>Nerve or spinal-cord compression</li><li>Neck injury and fracture</li></ul></article>
      <article class="directory__item" id="joints"><h2>Knee &amp; hip</h2><ul><li>Osteoarthritis</li><li>Meniscus and ligament injuries</li><li>Patellofemoral pain</li><li>Hip impingement and labral injury</li><li>Avascular necrosis</li><li>Fracture and dislocation</li></ul></article>
      <article class="directory__item" id="upper-limb"><h2>Shoulder &amp; elbow</h2><ul><li>Rotator cuff problems</li><li>Frozen shoulder and impingement</li><li>Shoulder instability</li><li>Tennis and golfer’s elbow</li><li>Cubital tunnel syndrome</li><li>Fracture and dislocation</li></ul></article>
      <article class="directory__item" id="hand-foot"><h2>Hand, wrist, foot &amp; ankle</h2><ul><li>Carpal tunnel syndrome</li><li>Trigger finger and tendon disorders</li><li>Ganglion cysts</li><li>Ankle sprains</li><li>Plantar fasciitis</li><li>Achilles tendon problems</li></ul></article>
      <article class="directory__item" id="trauma"><h2>Trauma &amp; fractures</h2><ul><li>Closed and open fractures</li><li>Joint dislocations</li><li>Sprains and soft-tissue injuries</li><li>Crush injuries</li><li>Sports injuries</li><li>Post-traumatic complications</li></ul></article>
      <article class="directory__item"><h2>Arthritis &amp; rheumatic pain</h2><ul><li>Osteoarthritis</li><li>Rheumatoid and psoriatic arthritis</li><li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li><li>Gout</li><li>Fibromyalgia</li><li>Inflammatory joint pain</li></ul></article>
      <article class="directory__item"><h2>Non-operative care</h2><ul><li>Physiotherapy and rehabilitation</li><li>Medication management</li><li>Bracing and supports</li><li>Lifestyle and ergonomic advice</li><li>Selected injections and nerve blocks</li><li>Home-care education</li></ul></article>
    </div></section>
    <section class="section section--dark"><div class="shell split"><div class="split__copy"><h2>Symptoms that need urgent assessment</h2><p>Seek urgent medical care after major trauma, or if pain is accompanied by new loss of bladder or bowel control, progressive weakness, numbness around the groin, fever with severe back pain, an open fracture, or a cold/pale limb.</p><p>This list is not exhaustive. If symptoms are severe or rapidly changing, use emergency services.</p><a class="btn btn--light" href="contact.html">Contact the clinic</a></div><figure class="split__media"><img src="assets/images/surgery.jpg" width="1080" height="608" loading="lazy" alt="Orthopaedic surgical team at work"></figure></div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><h2>How treatment is selected</h2><p>A named condition does not automatically determine treatment. Severity, duration, neurological findings, imaging, health history and your goals all matter.</p></div><div class="faq"><details><summary>When is imaging needed?</summary><p>Imaging is selected based on the examination and clinical question. Not every new episode of pain requires an MRI. Bring any existing X-rays, MRI or CT images and reports.</p></details><details><summary>Can I try non-operative treatment first?</summary><p>Often, yes. Appropriate care may include guided activity, physiotherapy, medication, bracing or injections. Some fractures, infections, neurological problems and unstable conditions may require faster intervention.</p></details><details><summary>When should I consider a second opinion?</summary><p>A second opinion is reasonable before major elective surgery, when the diagnosis is uncertain, or when treatment choices carry different risks and recovery paths.</p></details></div></div></section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
